<section
  id='case-studiobid'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: StudioBid | Web Development | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='A JavaScript auction platform built with API integration, authentication, bidding, credits, listings, profiles, and reusable UI states.'
  data-case='studiobid'
  aria-labelledby='case-studiobid-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-studiobid-title'>StudioBid</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          An API-driven auction platform for browsing, selling, and bidding on creative work.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI design and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Semester Project 2</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>JavaScript, Bootstrap, Sass, Noroff API</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Complete auction platform</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='studiobid'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='StudioBid sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='studiobid'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The task was to turn the Noroff Auction API into a working front-end application. Anyone
        should be able to explore active listings. Registered users also needed to create auctions,
        place bids, update their profiles, and follow their available credits. These actions depend
        on authentication and live API data, so the interface had to make the user’s current status
        and available actions clear.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Auction data changes quickly. A successful bid can update the current price, the bid
            history, and the user’s remaining credits in a single action. Listings can also expire,
            and owners need different controls from bidders. Keeping those relationships accurate
            across listing cards, detail pages, forms, and profiles was the central technical
            challenge.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Allow public browsing while protecting actions that require an account</li>
            <li>Keep bids, credits, ownership, and listing status in sync with the API</li>
            <li>Make validation, loading states, and API errors clear without disrupting the task</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--browse'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-studiobid-600.webp'
            data-src='site-studiobid-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='StudioBid homepage showing search, sorting, auction cards, current bids, and time remaining'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Searchable auction listings with current bids and time remaining
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I separated API requests, authentication, rendering, and form handling into focused
              JavaScript modules. Shared behaviour stays in one place, while each page controls the
              logic that belongs to its own task.
            </li>
            <li>
              Authentication is stored between page visits and checked before protected actions.
              Visitors can still browse freely, but bidding, profile editing, and listing management
              only appear when the user has permission to use them.
            </li>
            <li>
              Listing cards focus on the information needed for an early decision: the current bid,
              time remaining, image, and listing title. The detail page adds the seller, full
              description, bid history, and relevant actions.
            </li>
            <li>
              Forms use shared validation and feedback helpers so fields behave consistently.
              Successful requests update the visible interface, while API errors explain what went
              wrong without clearing the user’s input.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--validation'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-studiobid-code-600.webp'
            data-src='site-studiobid-code-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='402'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Code editor showing a reusable StudioBid form validation helper for valid and invalid field states'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Reusable form validation and feedback logic</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        StudioBid brings public listings, authentication, bidding, credits, profiles, and auction
        management into one responsive application. Building it in vanilla JavaScript forced me to
        be deliberate about module boundaries and the flow of data between API requests and rendered
        views. The project also exposed a basic truth about transactional interfaces: the user needs
        to see the result of an action immediately. If a bid changes their credits but the page does
        not show it clearly, the code may be correct while the experience still feels uncertain.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>